  "common_questions": [
    {
      "q": "What is Disco-Bus?",
      "a": "A lightweight message dispatcher for AI agents. One agent sends, the dispatcher pushes to the recipient's listener immediately. No polling, no message broker. About 1000 lines of Python + Node, runs as systemd user services."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why not just use a message queue like Redis or RabbitMQ?",
      "a": "Redis and RabbitMQ are excellent but they're infrastructure — they require a broker process, client libraries, serialization conventions, and ops overhead. Disco-Bus is self-contained: Python stdlib + SQLite + a small Node MCP server. If it crashes, messages are already on disk. If an agent is offline, the inbox waits."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why not just use MCP tool calls between agents?",
      "a": "MCP tool calls are synchronous request-response. Disco-Bus is async fire-and-forget — an agent sends a message and continues working; the recipient handles it when ready. Agents also don't need to know each other's MCP endpoints, just their names."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if an agent is offline when a message arrives?",
      "a": "The dispatcher marks the envelope FAILED and the message stays in SQLite. When the recipient agent comes back online and reads its inbox, the message is there. No messages are lost — they just don't achieve DELIVERED state until the listener is up."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it require Discord?",
      "a": "No. Discord mirroring is entirely optional. Skip setup-discord.sh and the dispatcher runs the same — just without the human-readable channel. Discord is for observability, not delivery."
    },
    {
      "q": "How does agent identity work? Can an agent spoof another agent?",
      "a": "No. The from field is bound server-side to DISCOBUS_AGENT, set in each agent's systemd env file. The MCP client never passes from — it arrives from the environment. An agent can only send as itself."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does unread mean in the inbox API?",
      "a": "A message is 'unread' when the recipient hasn't replied to it. It's derived from the existing data — no extra schema field. If you reply to a message, the original is considered read. If you haven't replied, it shows up in ?unread_only=true."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I walk a conversation thread?",
      "a": "GET /mesh/thread/<any-message-id-in-the-chain> or use the thread MCP tool. The dispatcher walks reply_to back to the root via recursive CTE and returns all messages in order with {root_id, messages:[...]}. You don't need the root id — any message in the thread works."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I install it without prompts (CI / agent install)?",
      "a": "Fill out robot.install (a 22-line JSON manifest) and run ./robot-install.sh. It emits a single JSON result on stdout — {ok, steps{deps, config, npm, systemd, smoke_test}} — so you can parse success/failure without reading prose. Exit 0 = success."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it phone home?",
      "a": "No telemetry. The only outbound call is to Discord's API, and only when you've explicitly configured a bot token and enabled mirroring."
    }
